//===----------------------------------------------------------------------===//
// The traits that say something about a type without giving it a method.
//
// `Copy` is the one that means anything today. A type that implements it survives being
// assigned somewhere else, so `let b = a;` leaves `a` readable. Everything else moves,
// which is what keeps a tensor or a placed buffer linear: neither can declare `Copy`,
// since a type may only do so when all of its fields already have.
//
// The checker recognizes the trait by name, so it has to be declared somewhere a program
// can import rather than written out in each one. That is what this module is for.
//
// `Send` and `Sync` say how a value may cross tasks. The compiler derives both from the fields
// of a type, so a type does not implement them by hand. A raw pointer is neither, and a type that
// holds one opts in with `unsafe impl`.
//
// `Sized` is a no-op: every type Vx has is sized, and the bound exists to be written.

/// A type that is duplicated rather than moved when it is assigned.
///
/// Opt-in: a type is copyable only when it says so, and only when every field
/// already is. That is what keeps it away from placement -- a tensor or a placed buffer cannot
/// declare it, so they stay linear without a special case, and duplicating placed data stays an
/// explicit `transfer`.
///
/// Enforced for a struct and for a payload-free enum. A generic enum is not treated as linear
/// at all, so `Option` and `Result` survive a move whether or not they declare this (Vx#715).
trait Copy {}

/// The scalars already copy on every read. The impls are here so a generic body bounded by
/// `T : Copy` can be instantiated at one of them, which is what the bound is for.
macro_rules stamp_copy {
  ($t : ident) => {
    impl Copy for $t {}
  };
}

stamp_copy!(i8);
stamp_copy!(i16);
stamp_copy!(i32);
stamp_copy!(i64);
stamp_copy!(u8);
stamp_copy!(u16);
stamp_copy!(u32);
stamp_copy!(u64);
stamp_copy!(usize);
stamp_copy!(f32);
stamp_copy!(f64);
stamp_copy!(bool);

/// A type whose values may move into another task.
///
/// The compiler derives it: a struct or an enum is `Send` when each of its fields is. A raw
/// pointer and a closure type are not. A type that holds a raw pointer and is safe to move writes
/// `unsafe impl Send for T {}`, as `Vec` does.
trait Send {}

/// A type that two tasks may use through `&T` at the same time.
///
/// The compiler derives it as it derives `Send`. `SharedCell` is never `Sync`, because code can
/// write its value through `&`. An atomic holds a `SharedCell` and opts in with `unsafe impl`.
trait Sync {}

/// A type whose size is known at compile time, which every Vx type is. The bound exists to be
/// written; nothing is excluded by it.
trait Sized {}

/// A field that records a type without storing a value of it.
///
/// An empty struct occupies nothing, so a struct carrying one is no larger. It is how a type
/// parameter that appears in no field is still named by the type.
struct PhantomData<T> {
}
